WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.620
A template safety roadmap begins with the contract.

2
00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:12.920
Write down what each template expects from its type parameters, traits, policies, operations, ownership model, and failure behavior.

3
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:18.380
If the contract is important, make it visible in the API, not only in tribal knowledge.

4
00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:24.660
Use concepts, constraints, and static assertions to restrict templates to intended use.

5
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:29.640
Broad flexibility is valuable only when the design can actually support it.

6
00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:36.940
Clear compile-time failure is safer than accepting an instantiation that violates the template's invariants.

7
00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:41.140
Keep ownership and lifetime behavior explicit.

8
00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:47.240
Be careful with forwarding references, views, iterators, callbacks, and stored references.

9
00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:57.420
Control overload resolution and customization boundaries so reviewers understand which operations may run and which policy decisions may be specialized.

10
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:03.380
Avoid overly clever compile-time machinery when simpler code is clearer.

11
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.400
Monitor build cost and binary impact for template-heavy designs.

12
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:13.660
Govern specialization, traits, policy types, and extension points as normal security-relevant code.

13
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:24.580
Finally, test representative instantiations, include invalid-use checks where practical, enable helpful diagnostics, and review template-heavy code intentionally.

14
00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:35.400
Template security is disciplined generic API design: explicit contracts, constrained use, clear lifetime rules, governed customization, and evidence from tests.

15
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.200
That discipline scales.

16
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:46.980
The more widely a template is reused, the more valuable it becomes to document assumptions, constrain use, and preserve understandable diagnostics from the beginning.
